  <fragment slot="content">
    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>What This Calculator Shows</h2>
      <p>Enter your home's current <strong>market value</strong> and your local <strong>property tax rate</strong>. If you don't know your exact rate, use the <strong>state dropdown</strong> to select a state with a preset average effective tax rate. Enter the expected <strong>annual assessment growth rate</strong> and choose how many <strong>years to project forward</strong>.</p>
      <p>The results show your annual and monthly tax, the effective rate, total projected tax, and a year-by-year breakdown table.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Property Tax Formula</h2>
      <div class="formula-box">Annual Tax = Home Value x (Tax Rate / 100)</div>
      <p>For projected taxes: Projected Value = Current Value x (1 + Growth Rate)^n, where <strong>n</strong> = number of years in the future.</p>
    </div>

    <FAQ items={[
      { question: "How are property taxes calculated?", answer: "Property taxes are calculated by multiplying your home's assessed value by the local millage rate. The assessed value is determined by the county assessor and may be a percentage of your home's market value." },
      { question: "What is the average property tax rate in the US?", answer: "The average effective property tax rate in the United States is approximately 1.1% of home value. Rates vary significantly by state, from around 0.18% in Alabama and Hawaii to over 2.2% in Illinois, New Jersey, and Texas." },
      { question: "Can my property taxes increase each year?", answer: "Yes, property taxes can increase each year as your home's assessed value rises or if local tax rates increase. Many areas have annual assessment growth limits (like California's Prop 13 which caps increases at 2% per year)." },
      { question: "Are property taxes tax deductible?", answer: "Yes, property taxes paid on your primary residence are generally deductible if you itemize. However, the SALT deduction is capped at $10,000 per year." },
    ]} />

    <div class="content-section">
      <h2>Next Steps</h2>
      <p>Check your actual property tax rate on your county assessor's website — rates can vary within a state by city and school district. When budgeting for a home purchase, include property taxes, homeowner's insurance, and HOA fees alongside your mortgage payment. Research whether you qualify for any exemptions (homestead, senior, veteran, disability) that could reduce your bill. If you believe your assessment is too high, most jurisdictions allow you to file an appeal — and successful appeals can save hundreds or thousands per year.</p>
    </div>
  </fragment>

  <fragment slot="sidebar">
    <RelatedTools currentSlug="property-tax-calculator" category="real-estate" />
